76, "prior_sd": 5.11, "poll_margin": 3.8, "poll_sd": 8.86, "poll_weight": 0.25, "fundamentals": {"margin": 3.49, "lean": -3.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": 0.19}, "rating_margin": 5.6, "sigma_race": 5.8, "sigma_total": 6.5, "baseline_margin": 4.52, "analyst_adjustment": 0.28, "key_factors": ["Stelson leads the two most recent polls by about three points despite the district's Republican lean", "She has out-raised Perry and holds a cash-on-hand advantage", "Most race raters and prediction markets treat the Democrat as the favorite", "Thin polling and Perry's incumbency keep uncertainty elevated"], "rationale": "Recent polls, a clear fundraising edge, and a broad consensus of raters and markets all point to Stelson in a Democratic-favorable midterm environment; the district's Republican tilt and Perry's incumbency are the main counterweights, keeping this a lean rather than likely Democratic outcome.", "watch": "Fresh polling showing Perry back in front, or a late shift in the national environment toward Republicans, would move the race back toward toss-up.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Scott Perry (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Pennsylvania's 10th District pairs a Republican-leaning electorate with a well-funded Democratic challenge to incumbent Scott Perry. Janelle Stelson has out-raised Perry and holds more cash on hand, and the two most recent polls show her ahead by about three points. Most race raters call the contest a Democratic lean or better, with two calling it a toss-up, and prediction markets price her chances around eighty percent.\n\nThe district's Republican tilt and Perry's incumbency remain real obstacles, and the polling record is thin, so uncertainty stays wide. A favorable national environment for Democrats is the main force pushing the race toward the challenger. The forecast expects Stelson to win by a modest margin, with enough variance that an incumbent hold would not be a surprise.", "history": [{"date":